  "articleBody": "Nobody wants plumbing rough-in problems during a remodel, right? Here’s a quick checklist to help you dodge those common headaches and keep your project on track. First, make sure your general contractor reviews all the plumbing drawings super early. This helps catch potential conflicts with other trades before they become costly issues. Second, lock in all your fixture and valve selections before the rough-in even begins. Seriously, waiting on this can cause major delays and compatibility issues. Third, get the work sequence aligned with other trades, especially electrical and HVAC, to avoid them trying to occupy the same space at the same time. Fourth, any changes to the scope? Get them in writing! This keeps everyone on the same page and prevents misunderstandings down the line. Finally, confirm your rough-in before any walls or ceilings get closed up. This is a critical step to ensure everything is correct and accessible. If you're feeling overwhelmed, that's totally understandable. When projects get complex, with lots of hidden rough-in work, having a professional like HH Remodeling LLC to manage this for you can prevent costly rework and keep your renovation moving forward smoothly.",
